records=[
('and-or-tree-disagreement-contracts','P0033','lemma','Quantitative disagreement contraction for the binary/ternary AND/OR tree','probability.recursive-trees','For a Galton-Watson tree with P(D=2)=3/5 and P(D=3)=2/5, apply AND at degree 2 and OR at degree 3 to independent Bernoulli(1/2) spins at depth n. If q_n is the conditional root-one probability given the truncated unmarked tree, then d_n=2 E[q_n(1-q_n)] satisfies d_0=1/2 and d_(n+1)=0.9 d_n-0.6 d_n^2+0.1 d_n^3 <=0.9 d_n. Thus E[q_n(1-q_n)]<=0.25*(0.9)^n, and the limiting conditional root probability is a measurable binary colour with both colours of probability 1/2. The truncated-tree Bayes colour-prediction error is at most 0.5*(0.9)^n; exact arithmetic gives variance at most 1e-6 at depth 118 and prediction-error bound at most 1e-6 at depth 125.',['Independent GW child subtrees','Independent Bernoulli(1/2) boundary spins']),
('and-or-same-colour-flow-stationarity','P0033','lemma','The AND/OR same-colour selector preserves GW and is nonergodic','probability.recursive-trees','For GW offspring P(D=2)=3/5, P(D=3)=2/5, use the measurable limiting AND/OR colour obtained from Bernoulli(1/2) boundary spins. Choosing uniformly among children with the root colour preserves the unmarked GW law. The colour is invariant along the selected-subtree chain and has both values with mass 1/2, so that stationary chain is nonergodic. The rule extends equivariantly and in a Borel manner to all infinite locally finite rooted trees by choosing uniformly among infinite children off the GW-full good set. Multiplying successive transition weights gives a consistent flow.',['Zero weights to wrong-coloured children are permitted','Stationary law is GW itself']),
('strict-discount-zero-mark-frechet-nonendogeny','P0355','lemma','Strict-discount zero-mark Frechet fixed points are nonendogenous','probability.recursive-trees','The deterministic binary recursion X=(1/2) max(X_1,X_2) has, for every a>0, the nonminimal fixed distribution P(X<=x)=exp(-a/x) for x>0 and zero for x<=0. Its invariant recursive tree process is nonendogenous because all innovations are deterministic while the root law is nondegenerate. The r-th moment is finite exactly for 0<r<1. This is a zero-additive-mark special case, not the positive-mark conjecture.',['IID children at each finite boundary','All innovations deterministic','a>0']),
('max-lipschitz-r-moment-endogeny','P0355','lemma','A max-Lipschitz moment contraction implies endogeny','probability.recursive-trees','For an invariant deterministic b-ary recursive tree process X=g(xi;X_1,...,X_b), assume the usual independent child root laws and innovation independence, and |g(xi;x)-g(xi;y)|<=c max_i|x_i-y_i| with 0<c<1. If E|X|^r is finite for some r>0 and b*c^r<1, then X is measurable from all innovations. Conditional root-copy differences have r-th moment at most (b*c^r)^n E|X-X_prime|^r after revealing n generations. For the binary c=1/2 Frechet fixed points, finite moments require r<1 while contraction requires r>1, so this criterion never applies.',['Finite r-th root moment','b*c^r<1','Standard invariant RTP independence assumptions']),
('normalised-moment-measures-determine-random-measure-limit','C0075','lemma','All normalised moment measures determine a random probability-measure limit','probability.random-measures','Let S be Polish and mu_n, mu be random Borel probability measures on S. If E[mu_n^{tensor r}] converges weakly to E[mu^{tensor r}] on S^r for every integer r>=1, then mu_n converges in law to mu in P(S) with the weak topology. First-moment convergence yields tightness of the random-measure laws by Markov bounds on compact complements. All mixed moments of bounded continuous integral vectors then determine their compactly supported laws. Every normalised r-th moment measure has total mass exactly one; no factorial-mass or Carleman argument is needed.',['Probability measures, not unnormalised cluster counting measures','All integer orders r>=1','Polish state space']),
('three-moment-measures-do-not-determine-law','C0075','lemma','First three moment measures can agree while the random-measure laws differ','probability.random-measures','On S={0,1}, let mu_P=(1-P) delta_0+P delta_1. Compare P uniform on [0,1] with P taking 0,1/2,1 with probabilities 1/6,2/3,1/6. Their random probability measures have identical first, second and third moment measures, but the fourth moments of their mass at 1 are 1/5 and 5/24, differing by 1/120. Therefore finitely many low-order moment-measure limits cannot certify an ISE random-measure limit.',['Only finite-moment sufficiency is refuted; no percolation theorem is refuted']),
('ise-hara-slade-three-point-source-scope','C0075','concept','Source scope of the conditioned Hara-Slade ISE calculation','probability.percolation','The cached Heydenreich-van der Hofstad survey, section 15.1, identifies the conditioned three-point calculation of Hara and Slade and describes higher-point convergence as missing in that source. The reference is T. Hara and G. Slade, The scaling limit of the incipient infinite cluster in high-dimensional percolation. II. Integrated super-Brownian excursion, J. Math. Phys. 41(3):1244-1293 (2000). Thus the September attempt did not establish all-order conditioned moment-measure convergence. A general moment-measure lemma remains a conditional reduction; it is not a literature solution or a current-openness certificate.',['Cached survey only; no fresh global literature verification']),
('attainable-peak-sets-are-independent','P0029','lemma','Attainable peak sets on finite connected graphs are precisely nonempty independent sets','probability.random-labelings','For a finite nonempty connected simple graph, a vertex set S is exactly the set of strict local maxima of some bijective vertex labeling if and only if S is nonempty and independent. Assign the largest labels to S and assign the other labels in decreasing order of distance from S to construct such a labeling. Consequently any nonadjacent two vertices can be the only peaks. On n-by-n grids both graph distances 2 (n>=3) and 2(n-1) occur with positive probability conditioned on two peaks. This support statement does not determine whether distance/n converges to zero.',['Distinct vertex labels','Connected finite graph']),
('two-peak-small-grid-exact-counts','P0029','concept','Exact two-peak counts on small rectangular grids','probability.random-labelings','Exhaustive uniform-permutation enumeration gives 8 two-peak labelings out of 24 on the 2-by-2 grid, 424 out of 720 on the 2-by-3 grid, and 143808 out of 362880 on the 3-by-3 grid. On the 3-by-3 grid the graph-distance counts are 63552 at distance 2, 64512 at distance 3 and 15744 at distance 4. These are exact finite controls, with no asserted asymptotic separation law.',['Nearest-neighbour rectangular grid; strict local maxima']),
('continuous-cost-field-infinite-mean-supremum','P0229','lemma','Continuity and pointwise mean-length bounds do not ensure an integrable sampled supremum','probability.spatial-networks','There exists a random continuous finite nonnegative field L on the closed unit disk with L(0)=0, L(z)>=|z| and E L(z)<=3|z|, whose supremum has infinite expectation. Let P(J=j)=2^(-j), use disjoint continuous bumps of radius 2^(-j-5) centred at (1/2+2^(-j-2),0), and set L(z)=|z|+(2^J/J) psi_J(z). Its bump supremum has expectation sum_j 1/j. An independent infinite IID uniform sample is dense and has the same supremum. This field is not asserted to satisfy compatible-route or SIRSN axioms.',['Random continuous cost fields only; no route-geometry counterexample claimed']),
('spatial-lp-increments-give-integrable-sample-supremum','P0229','lemma','Spatial Lp increments with p greater than two give an integrable sampled supremum','probability.spatial-networks','Let L be jointly measurable on a bounded square containing the unit disk, with E|L(z_0)| finite at one anchor and E|L(x)-L(y)|^p<=C|x-y|^p for all x,y and some p>2. Then dyadic chaining gives a continuous modification with finite expected absolute supremum; at level k the expected maximum parent-child and neighbour increment is O(2^(-k*(1-2/p))). Independence and Fubini identify that modification with L at every point of an infinite IID uniform sample, simultaneously almost surely, so the sampled absolute supremum is integrable. Deriving the increment hypothesis for actual routes is a separate task.',['Joint measurability','Uniform p-th increment bound with p>2','Sample independent of field']),
('bounded-common-gaussian-mode-shifts-extrema','C0008','lemma','A bounded common Gaussian covariance mode changes centred extremal limits','probability.gaussian-fields','Let Y_N(x)=X_N(x)+Z for a finite Gaussian field X_N and independent Z~N(0,sigma^2), sigma>0, shared by every site. All covariance entries change by sigma^2. For any deterministic centring m_N, max Y_N-m_N=max X_N-m_N+Z, and if the latter X maximum tends in law to a real M, the Y maximum tends to M+Z, a different law. Heights and level-set thresholds translate exactly by Z. Thus a uniformly bounded covariance difference alone cannot force identical extremal limit laws. This changes the off-diagonal finite covariance limit and need not meet a specified continuum Green-function hypothesis.',['Independent nonzero common Gaussian mode','No disproof of the full Problem 16.8 hypotheses']),
('susceptibility-value-comparison-does-not-control-log-derivative','C0067','lemma','Uniform value comparison does not control a logarithmic-derivative maximiser','probability.percolation','For V tending to infinity there are positive increasing C1 functions f_V and g_V on [0,1] with f_V<=g_V<=exp(0.1) f_V, for which the log derivative of f_V is maximised at 1/2 but that of g_V is outside every fixed K V^(-1/3) window around 1/2 for sufficiently large V. Set a=V^(1/3), f_V=a exp(arctan(a(p-1/2))) and g_V=f_V exp(S((p-3/4)V^2)/10), where S is the increasing cubic smoothstep from 0 to 1 with derivative 3/4 at 0. The added log derivative at 3/4 is (3/40)V^2. This is a calculus counterexample to a value-to-derivative inference, not a percolation susceptibility or a refutation of torus criticality.',['Constructed C1 functions; no actual percolation model asserted']),
('recurrent-z2-nonnegative-superharmonic-is-constant','C0098','lemma','Finite nonnegative superharmonic functions on recurrent Z2 are constant','probability.sandpiles','If u:Z^2->[0,infinity) is finite at every site and sum_{y~x}u(y)-4u(x)<=0 for every x, then u is constant. For nearest-neighbour simple random walk started at x, stopping on hitting y or at time n gives u(x)>=u(y) P_x(T_y<=n). Recurrence makes the probability tend to one, and reversing x and y proves equality. No boundedness or finite spatial average of u is assumed.',['Recurrence of nearest-neighbour Z2 simple random walk','Pointwise finiteness and nonnegativity']),
('sandpile-three-four-face-is-decidable','C0098','lemma','The iid sandpile face supported on heights three and four is decidable','probability.sandpiles','Under a fair legal Z2 sandpile schedule with toppling threshold 4, every configuration at least 3 everywhere with a height-4 seed has every site topple infinitely often. A finite nonnegative odometer would be superharmonic everywhere and strictly so at the seed, contradicting recurrence. Infinite topplings then propagate to neighbours. Thus an IID law on {3,4} has infinite toppling probability zero if P(4)=0 and one if P(4)>0. For rationally specified probabilities, testing P(4)=0 gives a finite decision algorithm on this face; for arbitrary real inputs this is a probability criterion without a claimed computable zero test. Every law on {0,1,2,3} is already stable. In particular the stable background of height 3 plus one extra chip need not stabilise; a blanket stable-background-plus-finite-perturbation claim is false.',['Nearest-neighbour Z2, threshold 4, fair legal dynamics','Rationally specified probabilities for the algorithm claim','No general five-probability algorithm claimed']),
('fk-positive-q-monotone-coupling-question','C0146','concept','Random-cluster monotone coupling question for positive q below one','probability.random-cluster','For a finite graph with free boundary, FK(p,q) assigns an edge set A probability proportional to p^|A|(1-p)^(|E|-|A|) q^k(A), where k(A) includes isolated components. For 0<q<1, the cited Question 13.2 asks for an inclusion-monotone joint realisation for all p in [0,1] on general graphs. The source also mentions q=0; that endpoint needs a separate limiting convention and is not settled by positive-q formulae. This is a historical problem statement, not a fresh global status claim.',['q>0 for the normalised probability formula','Free boundary conditions']),
('finite-cactus-fk-monotone-p-coupling','C0146','lemma','Finite cactus random-cluster graphs have a monotone coupling in p for every positive q','probability.random-cluster','For every finite simple cactus graph with free boundary conditions and q>0, the FK(p,q) laws have a simultaneous inclusion-monotone coupling for all p in [0,1]. The component count is |V|-|A| plus the number of completely open edge-disjoint cycles, so the law factors over bridge and cycle blocks. Bridges are Bernoulli(p/(p+q(1-p))). A cycle block is a uniform K-subset given K, with K mass proportional to binomial(m,k) (p/(q(1-p)))^k q^(1_{k=m}). Likelihood-ratio order couples K monotonically by one uniform quantile; one independent uniform edge permutation nests the subsets. This includes 0<q<1 and uses no FKG.',['Finite simple cactus graph','Free boundary; positive q','No q=0 claim']),
('fk-small-graph-exact-increasing-event-derivative-controls','C0146','concept','Exact increasing-event derivative controls for small random-cluster graphs','probability.random-cluster','The w331 finite run tested q in {1/10,1/4,1/2,3/4,9/10,1} and p in {1/10,1/4,1/2,3/4,9/10} on a path, a triangle, two triangles sharing one vertex, K_(2,3) and K4. At all 150 graph/parameter pairs, an exact integer minimum-cut calculation over all increasing events found no negative derivative of FK event probability in p. The triangle runs were checked against 600 brute-force upward-closed events, and an artificial negative derivative control was detected. These rational point checks do not prove monotonicity at every p,q or coupling on arbitrary graphs.',['Exact finite parameter evidence only']),
('sierpinski-carpet-boundary-remainder-has-a-segment','P0021','lemma','A Sierpinski-carpet boundary remainder contains a nontrivial segment','probability.brownian-geometry','Let K be the standard Sierpinski carpet in the unit square. Remove the boundaries of all connected components of R^2 minus K. The remaining set contains {(1/4,y):0<y<1}, hence is not totally disconnected, although K is compact, connected and has area zero. The number 1/4 has ternary expansion 0.020202..., lies in the Cantor set and is not a gap endpoint; every deleted-square horizontal projection is contained in a closed Cantor gap, so the segment avoids all hole boundaries and the outer boundary. This deterministic set is not claimed to be a Brownian trace.',['Standard planar Sierpinski carpet','Generic topology only; no Brownian counterexample']),
('brownian-remainder-needs-spatial-geometry','P0021','concept','Brownian trace remainder requires a spatial geometric argument','probability.brownian-geometry','Neither zero area and connectedness of the original compact set nor total disconnectedness of a surviving time set alone establishes total disconnectedness of a planar Brownian spatial remainder. The Sierpinski-carpet boundary deletion retains a segment, and a continuous map of a totally disconnected compact time set can map onto an interval, as the Cantor function does. The Brownian assertion therefore needs geometry specific to the spatial trace; these obstructions do not answer it.',['No current-status or solution claim'])]
